  "deletedAt": null,
  "details": "In many graph languages these days, they include a \"portal\" feature to help with unwieldy, complex, and long graphs. \n\nWith the new addition of groups, it is very useful for organizing complex graphs, however, the issue arises when you want to use outputs of these groups to other groups or sections of functionality. This would in effect create long and unsightly noodle connections which are hard to manage, harder to read, harder to understand, and more error prone because of that. If you have to ask yourself, \"what is this connecting to?\", because it's off of the screen, it's a bad sign.\n\nPortals solve these issues by allowing the output of one node to effectively enter a \"portal\", and it can connect to the same named exit portal which then connects to a nodes input, all while hiding the noodle and at the same time telling you which portal it is, thus allowing you to know where it's coming from without following a really long noodle connection.\nIn my opinion, Gaea did it the best, as can be seen below.",
